# Bundle app

Requires Bun 1.4.4 or newer. This app uses a local `file:` dependency on the Bundle checkout that generated it. Keep that checkout available; this is not a published framework package. If you move it, update the dependency and reinstall. No dependencies are installed during generation.

```bash
bun install
bun run dev       # Vite on http://127.0.0.1:5173 and Bundle on http://127.0.0.1:3000
```

After the initial migration below, open the Vite URL, choose **Create an account**, register with an ASCII email and a password of at least 12 characters, then create a note. **Log out** clears the account, list and draft; **Log in** restores that account's notes. **Refresh notes** retries failed reads. The UI restores an existing cookie session on load. It shows loading, empty, success and fixed error messages, never raw server errors. The static typed manifest is `src/web/routes.ts`. Routes default to GET/HEAD; notes explicitly declares GET and POST (GET also enables HEAD). Explicit declarations support GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE and OPTIONS; HEAD is only an implicit GET alias. Omitted methods still default to GET/HEAD. OPTIONS is explicit only, with no automatic OPTIONS/CORS response. Unsupported methods return 405 with the route’s precise Allow header in GET, HEAD, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE, OPTIONS order, before middleware or handlers run. For example, `{ path: "/items/:id", methods: ["PATCH", "DELETE"], handler }` permits only those two verbs and returns `Allow: PATCH, DELETE` for others. Unknown paths retain GET/HEAD 404 and other-method 405 with `Allow: GET, HEAD`. `make:route --register` still generates GET/HEAD defaults and recognizes unchanged historical generated manifest headers; manually edited registrations require manual manifest edits thereafter. `scripts/dev.ts` supervises both processes. Ctrl-C or SIGTERM stops both process trees; an unexpected exit (including a clean child exit) stops its sibling and exits nonzero. Shutdown allows 500 ms before forcing remaining children to exit. The Bundle server does not automatically restart on file changes. `bun run server` still runs Bundle alone. The Notes API provides authenticated, user-owned create/list; update/delete are not part of this slice.

Override development ports together with `BUNDLE_PORT=3100 VITE_PORT=5174 bun run dev`; Vite forwards `/api` to the chosen Bundle port. Both default to loopback; Vite fails if its port is occupied. Port values must be integers from 1 to 65535. Vite options can be passed after `bun run dev --`. Process-tree shutdown uses process groups on POSIX; Windows stops the direct children.

```bash
bun run bundle list
bun run bundle inspect --json
bun run bundle make:model Product --field title:string --field price:number --field 'description:text?'
bun run bundle make:migration create_auth_notes
bun run db:migrate
bun run bundle db:seed
bun run bundle db:show
bun run bundle make:route Example
bun run bundle make:request CreatePost --field title:string --field 'published:boolean?'
bun run typecheck
bun run test
bun run build
bun run preview
```

Generate and review the initial migration before migrating or seeding. SQLite defaults to this app's `.data/bundle.sqlite`. Commands use this app's root, models, migrations and seeders. `make:route` prints registration snippets; edit the manifest explicitly. `make:page` remains a separate server-rendered TypeScript generator, not a React page generator.

`build` produces frontend assets in `dist/`. `preview` previews only those assets and does not proxy APIs. `bun run start:production` serves the generated `dist/` frontend and registered API routes together; it requires a successful build first. TLS termination and any external CDN/proxy configuration remain the operator's responsibility.

The account and Notes UI uses same-origin `/api/auth/register`, `/api/auth/login`, `/api/auth/me`, `/api/auth/logout`, `/api/auth/password` and `/api/notes` calls through the Vite proxy. It uses only the HTTP-only session cookie; no tokens are exposed to JavaScript or stored in browser storage. Sign-in password inputs are cleared after submission. Client validation follows the server's email/password/body limits, including UTF-8 password bytes and the 4 KiB credential JSON limit. Notes display as text, up to 100 oldest-first, with a 1000-character editor. Creating re-reads the bounded list, so a new note beyond the first 100 is saved but outside the visible window. A failed write may already have saved: refresh before retrying.

Session changes clear private state, abort requests and invalidate late completions. Expired Notes requests return to sign-in. If session restoration fails, retry it before signing in. If logout fails, private state is cleared and account changes stay blocked until **Retry logout** succeeds; closing the page alone does not revoke the server session. Choose **Account** to open the labelled **Change password** form. Enter current, new and matching confirmation passwords. Both submitted passwords require 12–1024 JavaScript string units and at most 1024 UTF-8 bytes; the serialized JSON must fit 4 KiB. Values are sent exactly as typed, without trimming or normalization, and never stored in local/session storage. Success clears all three fields and keeps this session using the browser-applied rotated HTTP-only cookie; other sessions are revoked. A wrong-current-password 401 triggers `/api/auth/me` revalidation and preserves a still-valid account. Only a revalidation 401 confirms expiry; network, server and invalid-response failures show a fixed safe error. Pending submissions are locked, late responses are bound to the session epoch, and logout/account changes clear the form. Account also includes **Sessions**, showing minimal ID/expiry metadata and a current marker, capped at 50 active sessions, newest first. **Revoke session** signs out another session and refreshes the list; use **Log out** for the current session. The UI validates exact fields, safe IDs, dates, list size/order and current markers before rendering, and shows generic errors. Closing Account, logging out, changing accounts or changing the session clears the list and invalidates late responses. No user-agent or IP data is collected. Recovery UI is not included.

The frontend uses React 19, Vite 8 and Tailwind CSS v4 via `@tailwindcss/vite`. CSS theme tokens live in `src/index.css`; no Tailwind v3 config or PostCSS plugin is needed. `components.json`, the `@/` alias, `cn` utility and editable Radix/CVA Button follow shadcn/ui conventions (new-york, neutral). The shadcn CLI is not installed or executed. Additional registry components may require additional dependencies. Source files belong to you.

Framework maintainers can run the short-lived dev integration test from the Bundle checkout with `BUNDLE_DEV_NODE_MODULES=/absolute/path/to/installed/app/node_modules bun test src/cli/app-dev.test.ts`. It reserves two free loopback ports, checks the frontend and proxied health response, sends SIGTERM, and verifies both ports can be rebound. Socket denial fails the integration test explicitly. This reuses dependencies and does not validate installation.


After generating the models' initial migration with the CLI, review the SQL (unique `users.email`, unique `sessions.token_hash`, expiry index, and non-null Note owner foreign key), then apply it. No shared account or demo notes are seeded. Existing apps with unowned notes need a reviewed data migration assigning a real owner before making the relation non-null; do not silently assign old notes to the first registrant.

Auth endpoints (JSON responses contain only `{id, email}`):

- `POST /api/auth/register`: exactly `{"email":"alice@example.com","password":"a long unique password"}`, returns 201 and a session cookie.
- `POST /api/auth/login`: the same schema, returns 200 and a replacement cookie. Bad credentials return a generic 401; duplicate registration returns a generic 400.
- `POST /api/auth/password`: exactly `{"currentPassword":"a long unique password","newPassword":"a different unique password"}` with a live session cookie. Returns 204 and a rotated cookie; wrong current password returns generic 401 `Invalid credentials.` without changing credentials or sessions. Both passwords use the length/byte bounds below; extra fields are rejected.
- `GET /api/auth/me`: returns the signed-in user, or 401.
- `GET /api/auth/sessions`: authenticated, returns an array of exactly `{id,expiresAt,current}` entries, up to 50 unexpired sessions in descending ID order (newest first). Metadata is minimal: ID, ISO expiry and current-session marker only. No token hashes, credentials, user-agent or IP data are returned; no user-agent/IP data is collected. The current session may fall outside the newest 50.
- `DELETE /api/auth/sessions/:id`: authenticated, exact configured Origin required before session/database lookup. The ID must be a canonical positive safe integer. Only the signed-in user's unexpired session can be revoked. Current session returns safe 409 (use logout instead); foreign, missing and expired sessions all return the same safe 404. Success returns 204. Both endpoints use no-store and accept no caller-selected user ID.
- `POST /api/auth/logout`: revokes the presented token and clears the cookie; returns 204, including when already signed out.

Every POST requires an `Origin` header exactly equal to `APP_PUBLIC_ORIGIN`; missing, `null`, and foreign origins return 403, including for logout and Notes. Set this environment value to the browser-facing origin (scheme, host, optional port; no trailing slash or path). Production (`NODE_ENV=production` or `APP_ENV=production`) requires an explicit HTTPS origin. Outside production only, the default is `http://127.0.0.1:${VITE_PORT ?? 5173}` using configured environment values, never request Host/forwarded headers. For a browser opened on localhost, configure `APP_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173` instead. An authenticated same-origin browser sends the cookie automatically; direct clients must send both `Cookie` and `Origin` on mutations. There is no cross-origin credential/CORS support.

Email policy is exact: reject non-ASCII input and raw input over 320 characters, trim only edge ASCII space/tab/CR/LF, then lowercase ASCII. The resulting email is at most 254 characters, with a 1–64 character dot-atom local part and DNS-style domain with at least two labels. Leading/trailing/repeated local dots, empty domain labels, and invalid hyphens are rejected. No Unicode normalization, provider aliasing, or plus/dot rewriting is performed. Email uniqueness uses the normalized value. Passwords are preserved exactly, require at least 12 JavaScript string characters and at most 1024 UTF-8 bytes. Auth JSON is limited to 4 KiB. Passwords use Bun.password Argon2id (64 MiB, two iterations, random salt); only the hash is stored. See [Bun's password API](https://bun.sh/guides/util/hash-a-password).

Sessions use 32 cryptographically random bytes encoded as an opaque base64url token; SQLite stores only its SHA-256 hash, a user foreign key and an absolute seven-day expiry. A single `bundle_session` cookie is HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, Path=/, has no Domain, and is Secure in production. Login/register rotate the token and transactionally revoke the token presented with the request; other devices' sessions remain valid. Password change atomically updates the Argon2id credential, deletes every prior session for the user (including other devices), and stores a fresh hashed session token. It rechecks the session and conditionally updates the verified hash inside the transaction so an overlapping change cannot overwrite a newer credential. A failed write rolls back the credential and session changes; unexpected database failures return generic 500 with no cookie. Logout deletes the presented session. Expired and revoked tokens return 401. Tokens are reusable bearer credentials until expiry/revocation, not one-time request nonces. Do not log passwords, cookies, tokens, or request bodies; keep proxy/access logging redacted too. Clean up expired session rows periodically as an operational task.

`POST /api/notes` requires a session, Content-Type application/json and exactly `{"body":"your note"}`. The parser rejects malformed JSON (400), other media types (415), and payloads over 16 KiB UTF-8 (413). Unknown fields, nonstrings, and trimmed bodies outside 1–1000 JavaScript string characters return 400. Success returns 201 with `{id, body}`. `GET /api/notes` lists only the current user's notes, at most 100 ordered by ascending ID; HEAD returns no body. Ownership comes from the session, never client input. Responses include no-store and router security headers; unexpected errors return generic 500. Stored text is JSON data; escape it for its rendering context.

Handlers resolve this app's root from the module URL and use Bundle `withDatabase` with a forked entity manager per request. Tests route through `createRoutes(env)` from the actual manifest against temporary real SQLite databases, retaining direct Auth and Notes body-validation checks. The framework's `scripts/verify-app.ts` (`BUN_INSTALL_CACHE_DIR` for a populated cache, `BUNDLE_NPM_REGISTRY` for a cached registry, `BUNDLE_INSTALL_OFFLINE=1` to forbid network) additionally installs the generated app, typechecks/tests/builds it, checks rendered accessible account controls, generates and applies its migration through the CLI, and exercises register/login/me/password-change/create/list/logout on the migrated database.

This is a **development-auth milestone, not production auth readiness**. The generated manifest now statically injects the bounded SQLite rolling limiter when `NODE_ENV=production` or `APP_ENV=production`; the dedicated production command performs database and origin preflight before loading routes or binding a listener. Direct `createRoutes()` use in production still rejects absent or process-scoped limiters. Production auth still needs operational review, public service policy, recovery and broader controls.

### Production startup and limiter

The generated `src/auth-attempt-limiter.ts` supplies `createDatabaseAuthAttemptLimiter(env)`, an opt-in-to-production static SQLite implementation of `AuthAttemptLimiter`. The generated production route manifest injects it only when `NODE_ENV=production` or `APP_ENV=production`. The adapter uses the app's existing Bundle database configuration and `withDatabase` lifecycle; no package or separate provider is required. Normal development keeps its bounded process-local limiter.

Run production from the generated app:

```bash
bun run bundle make:migration initial
# Review generated migration source/SQL before applying.
bun run db:migrate
bun run build
APP_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://your-host NODE_ENV=production bun run start:production
```

`start:production` is a separate explicit entrypoint. Build the frontend first with `bun run build`. Before loading routes or opening a listener, it requires an exact HTTPS `APP_PUBLIC_ORIGIN`, persistent SQLite (not `:memory:`), a regular non-symlink database file, all discovered model tables, applied migration history with no pending migrations, the exact bounded auth-limiter schema, and a regular `dist/index.html` build tree with only recognized static file types and no symlinks. It never creates a database, applies migrations, or falls back to process-local limiting. Missing readiness fails closed with safe guidance. Keep the database configuration identical for migration and runtime.

The app handler sends `/api` requests only to the fixed route manifest; unknown API paths remain 404 and never fall through to the SPA. Other GET/HEAD requests serve the captured Vite build, using an index fallback only for extensionless SPA paths. Built assets use a restrictive same-origin CSP and `nosniff`; `index.html` and unhashed assets are no-store, while fingerprinted `/assets/` files may be immutable for one year. `GET/HEAD /api/health` is a liveness probe and does not touch the database. `GET/HEAD /api/ready` runs a bounded `select 1` against the configured database and returns only `{"status":"ready"}` or a generic 503 `{"status":"unavailable"}`; it never exposes connection errors and is no-store. Production startup separately verifies migrations and required schema before binding. The server defaults to loopback at `127.0.0.1:3000`. `BUNDLE_HOST` may override this only with a numeric IPv4/IPv6 address; `BUNDLE_PORT` must be 1–65535. Keep loopback behind a TLS-terminating reverse proxy unless you have deliberately reviewed public binding. TLS termination, API forwarding and any external CDN are operator responsibilities; startup serves the prebuilt assets but does not run Vite.

The bundled limiter supports SQLite only and requires a shared persistent file with reliable SQLite locking across workers. Independent local files are separate limiters; do not assume network filesystem locking is safe. It refuses PostgreSQL, MariaDB and other drivers. The generated `AuthAttemptBucket` model remains portable across normal ORM schema generation, but portability does not enable this limiter on server databases; those require a separately reviewed adapter. Direct production consumers of `createRoutes` must explicitly inject a genuinely shared limiter; the scope marker is an assertion, not proof of shared storage.

The table has one fixed primary key (`id = 1`) and exactly twenty non-null integer epoch-millisecond columns (`slot_0` through `slot_19`), ordered oldest to newest; zero means unused. Portable checks enforce the singleton, safe nonnegative range and ordering. The SQLite limiter additionally validates every persisted slot as a nonnegative safe integer before admitting requests; fractional or text values fail closed even if checks were bypassed. No user, IP, credential or request identity is stored. Only the latest twenty admissions persist; each admission shifts the nineteen retained slots and overwrites the oldest, so storage never grows with requests or identities. No cleanup job is needed.

Login and registration share **at most 20 admitted attempts in any rolling 60-second interval**, including malformed credentials after Origin validation. The oldest retained timestamp must be more than 60,001 DB milliseconds old before reuse. The conservative one-millisecond margin covers SQLite's rounded millisecond clock, including the exact cutoff; this is an exact timestamp policy with a small conservative expiry delay, not approximate time buckets. Denials leave all slots unchanged and return rounded-up positive `Retry-After` (up to 61 seconds) with 429/no-store. Twenty timestamps suffice: when twenty admissions are still in the interval, deny; once the oldest expires, every earlier discarded admission is also outside it. The global policy can deny service to all users.

Each consume opens the configured app DB, begins a transaction, conflict-safely inserts the singleton as its **first statement** (reserving SQLite's writer lock even when the row exists), reads the row and database clock after locking, and checks/updates all slots before committing. All workers serialize through that write reservation, including cold-row creation; process restarts reuse committed timestamps. There is no process clock or request-derived time. Backward DB-clock adjustments clamp to the latest stored timestamp to preserve ordering and conservatively delay admissions; forward jumps can expire state early relative to real elapsed time. The guarantee is in database time and requires a stable authoritative DB clock; it is not a monotonic real-time guarantee across clock corrections.

SQLite busy conflicts roll back before at most 20 short asynchronous retries (roughly two seconds total); native blocking busy waits are disabled on that operation's connection. Exhausted retries, missing/old migrations, connection errors, invalid state and transaction/write/commit failures reject `consume()`. Auth fails closed with generic 500 before credential processing, without a process-local fallback or request-time schema changes. A committed admission counts even if subsequent auth work fails. Keep constraints intact and restrict DB writes: deleting/resetting/restoring state changes the guarantee.

This trades a wider row and twenty bound timestamp writes per admitted request for precise expiry and bounded state. The integer payload is at most 160 bytes for timestamps (plus ID, column headers, B-tree/page/journal overhead; typical current epoch values use six SQLite payload bytes each). CPU work is O(20), with no growing history scan, JSON parsing or cleanup. Every consume, including denial, reserves SQLite's single writer and uses the existing per-call connection lifecycle, so unrelated DB writes and concurrent auth contend. This is suitable only where measured throughput and lock/error budgets fit; no throughput claim is made.

For an existing fixed-window deployment, stop all consumers and review a generated SQLite migration replacing `attempts`/`expires_at` with these required slots/checks **before** starting the new adapter. The old count cannot reconstruct admission timestamps. A reviewed conversion can conservatively fill all twenty slots with the old `expires_at` converted to integer milliseconds (round up), preserving denial through at least its expiry plus a full minute; alternatively keep auth unavailable until more than sixty seconds after the last possible admission, then initialize zeros. Never silently reset a live bucket or run old and new writers together. Review generated table rebuilds/data conversion; the factory does not upgrade schema. New apps use normal `make:migration` / `migrate`.

`AuthAttemptLimiter` remains exported from `src/routes/Auth.ts` with readonly `scope: "process" | "shared"` and sync/async `consume()`. Alternative adapters must provide real atomic cross-process accounting, bounded storage, restart persistence and fail-closed behavior. The scope marker is an assertion, not verification. Production rejects `createProcessAuthAttemptLimiter()` even when explicitly supplied.

SQLite is verified against real isolated CLI-migrated databases: an actual 60-second boundary regression, concurrent factories and child processes, persistence after all workers exit, unchanged denied state, slot reuse, schema constraints, rollback/provider errors and production route injection. PostgreSQL/MariaDB are refused rather than offered with unverified live behavior. The real-time regression takes about a minute; acceptance gives the generated test suite a 120-second process budget.

A valid injected limiter does not relax required production HTTPS Origin configuration, cookie/session policies or route protection. It does **not** make production auth ready. Public use still needs a reviewed throttling policy and trusted client identity where applicable, concurrency controls, recovery and operational hardening. Password reset, email verification, MFA, account recovery and breached-password checking are not included. Keep the loopback binding for local work. Secure cookies and configuration gates alone do not establish deployment readiness.

Notes POST validates its body with `schema.object({ body: schema.string({ trim: true, min: 1, max: 1000 }) })`, imported from `bundle/src/validation/schema.ts`. Objects reject extra fields; `safeParse` returns a typed success or at most 20 safe `{ path, code }` issues. Notes calls `readValidatedJson(request, noteSchema, { maxBytes: 16 * 1024 })`, imported from `bundle/src/web/request.ts`, returning inferred data or a fixed 400 `Invalid request body` with no input or schema issues exposed. It reuses the streaming JSON/media/UTF-8 protections; `readJson` still returns unknown for manual validation. Auth credential/password changes use the helper with a 4096-byte cap while retaining `Invalid credentials.`, ASCII email semantic checks and unmodified password byte bounds. Use `schema.optional(...)` for optional fields, `schema.nullable(child)` for explicit null or the checked child output, and `Infer<typeof yourSchema>` for output types. Combine them as `schema.optional(schema.nullable(child))` when both omission and null are allowed.

`make:request CreatePost --field title:string --field 'published:boolean?'` creates `src/requests/CreatePost.ts`, exporting the strict `createPost` schema and inferred `CreatePost` type. It imports from the app's local `bundle/src/validation/schema.ts` dependency and is covered by the server typecheck. Repeat `--field name:type`; suffix the type with `?` for optional fields (omission/undefined, without accepting null). Strings trim to 1–255 characters; text trims to 1–10000; integers must be finite integer numbers without coercion; booleans are strict. Unknown object keys fail. At least one field is required, with at most 64 fields and 8192 source bytes. Names/specs/duplicates are validated before writes; existing entries are never overwritten, including with `--force`, and symlink directories are refused. Use the generated schema directly in a handler:

```ts
import { readValidatedJson } from "bundle/src/web/request.ts";
import { createPost } from "./requests/CreatePost.ts";

const data = await readValidatedJson(request, createPost, { maxBytes: 16 * 1024 });
// data.title is trimmed string; data.published is boolean | undefined.
```

The default cap is 64 KiB; `maxBytes` must be a positive safe integer. Malformed JSON/invalid UTF-8 return 400, unsupported media returns 415, and oversized bodies return 413, unchanged by schema validation. The helper exposes no issue paths/codes or values and accepts no custom public error message.

`make:model` accepts up to 64 repeated `--field name:type` options in argument order: `string`, `text`, `integer`, `number` (double), `boolean`, `date`, `json`. A trailing `?` makes the property optional for creation and nullable in SQL. No fields retains the id/timestamps scaffold. Names are bounded ASCII identifiers; reserved id/timestamp/object names, duplicates ignoring case/underscores, arbitrary types and source snippets are rejected before filesystem/database work. Existing entries and symlink directories are refused, even with `--force`; use trusted parent directories. Models are never overwritten.

Required `json` fields accept non-null JSON values at the root (nested nulls are supported). Use `json?` to allow SQL NULL or omission at the root, keeping required JSON fields required in MikroORM creation data.

After applying the initial migration baseline, `make:model Product --field title:string -m -s` also generates a diff and seeder. Migration generation runs after writing the model and never applies SQL. On migration generation failure, the error identifies the preserved model and seeder; fix the cause and retry `make:migration`. File publication is outside database transactions, so model/seeder/migration creation is not atomic.

Route middleware is optional: import `Middleware` and `Route` from `./src/web/router.ts`, then add `middleware: [audit, authorize]` to a route. A `Middleware` is `(request: Request, next: () => Promise<Response>, context?: RouteContext) => Response | Promise<Response>`. Handlers receive `context?: RouteContext` as argument two. Each router dispatch creates a fresh context; `createContextKey<T>(Symbol("name"))` creates an explicit typed key for `context.get(key)` (T or undefined), `context.set(key, value)` and `context.delete(key)` (boolean). Share key declarations across middleware and handlers; equal symbol descriptions do not make keys equal. Existing `(request, next)` middleware remains supported. Listed functions wrap the handler in order; await/return `next()` to continue or return an early response (for example 401) to skip later functions. Each `next()` dispatches once per request; repeats reject. Middleware arrays are validated and copied at router creation; later registration edits require a restart. Closures and application state remain mutable.

Middleware runs only for a matched route and allowed method, including the GET HEAD alias and every explicitly declared verb. It does not run for 404/405 or pages mode, or change exact static paths or Allow headers. The router applies security headers and suppresses/cancels the final HEAD body even for early responses. Unexpected errors and invalid responses produce generic secure 500; only supported `HttpError` instances expose intentionally public messages. Middleware is trusted application code with process privileges, not a sandbox or an automatic authorization guarantee. Validate credentials yourself, never put secrets in `HttpError` messages, and await/return `next()` rather than detaching it or calling it after returning.


The manifest explicitly mounts `requireAuth` on `/api/notes` for GET/HEAD/POST. Every mutation checks exact Origin before opening SQLite or looking up a session, including PATCH and DELETE on generated resources. Missing/expired sessions return 401 before Notes body validation and queries. Only `authenticatedUserId` (a number) is stored in context, never session IDs, hashes or cookie tokens. Notes uses an owner reference by ID without re-querying the user. Calling the Notes handler without authenticated context returns 401. Auth endpoints remain public as required by their individual policies; they do not inherit Notes middleware. Use `createRoutes(env)` to build the same manifest with isolated configuration in tests.


## SQLite queue and one-shot worker (early milestone)

The generated app includes the `bundle_jobs` model/table, `src/queue.ts` wrapper, a static `src/jobs/registry.ts`, and `bun run work:once` for claiming and handling at most one due job. For a new app, the normal reviewed initial migration creates the table. `make:job` generates a fail-closed handler skeleton and prints registration lines but deliberately does not edit the registry; implement the handler, then add both lines manually. Existing generated apps have no automated upgrade: copy the current queue/model/registry/worker files, then explicitly review a migration that updates the `bundle_jobs_failure_pair` constraint to permit `unregistered_job` and `invalid_payload`. Do not assume a model-diff migration rebuilds an existing SQLite CHECK constraint; inspect the generated SQL and verify the live schema before using the worker.

```sh
bun run bundle make:job SendWelcome --type mail.welcome --field email:string
```

The command creates `src/jobs/SendWelcome.ts` with an inferred payload schema and a TODO handler that throws until implemented. Its JSON response includes a fixed import and registry entry; add them to `src/jobs/registry.ts` after completing the handler. Use repeated `--field name:string|text|integer|number|boolean`; a trailing `?` permits omission. There is no dynamic module discovery.

```ts
import { createJobRegistry, defineJob } from "bundle/src/queue/worker.ts";
import { schema } from "bundle/src/validation/schema.ts";

export const jobs = createJobRegistry({
  "reports.generate": defineJob(
    schema.object({ reportId: schema.number({ integer: true, min: 1 }) }),
    async ({ reportId }) => { await buildReport(reportId); }, // app-owned operation
  ),
});
```

Enqueue work explicitly from application code:

```ts
import { queue } from "../queue.ts";

await queue.enqueue("default", "reports.generate", { reportId: 42 });
```

Dispatch a job with `bun run work:once`; it prints one compact JSON result (`idle`, `processed`, `retrying`, `failed`, or `lost`). `queue.enqueue(queueName, jobType, payload, { delayMs?, maxAttempts? })` writes bounded JSON (16 KiB), with a maximum 7-day delay and 1–20 attempts. `queue.claim(queueName?)` atomically reserves one due job, incrementing its attempt count. `queue.acknowledge(job)` deletes only the row still carrying that reservation token. `queue.fail(job)` records a generic failure code and retries with exponential delay (1 second up to 60 seconds); unregistered types and schema-invalid payloads fail terminally. No exception message is persisted. All time calculations use SQLite's database clock.

This remains **incomplete background processing**. There is no continuously polling worker, graceful worker supervisor/heartbeat, failed-job inspection/replay/pruning, or production operational policy. Delivery is at-least-once: handlers must be idempotent. The default lease is five minutes; a job continuing after lease expiry can be delivered again. The runtime refuses PostgreSQL and MariaDB even though the table model emits portable migration DDL; live queue behavior is SQLite-only. Use a persistent SQLite file with reliable locking; do not assume network-filesystem locking is safe. Payloads are ordinary database text—never enqueue passwords, session tokens or secrets, and minimize personal data. This is not production-ready background processing.

## Owner-scoped REST resources

```sh
bun run bundle make:resource Task --field title:string --field 'body:text?' --field done:boolean
# After the initial migration baseline: optionally add --migration / -m.
bun run bundle make:migration create_task
# Review SQL/source, then apply with this app CLI:
bun run bundle migrate
```

The authenticated App imports the exact static `src/resource-pages.tsx` registry. This command also generates an editable typed React page at `src/resource-pages/Task.tsx` and adds a visible Task link at `#/tasks`; Notes remains available at `#/notes`. App and existing pages are never overwritten. Keep the `ResourceNavigation` integration when customizing App. Older apps without this integration require a reviewed manual upgrade.

The page supports create/list, edit with PATCH, and delete. Lists show up to 100 records per page by ascending id, with accessible Previous/Next controls and a page indicator. Collection GET keeps its JSON array shape, accepts an optional exclusive `?after=ID` cursor and returns `X-Has-More: true|false` from a 101-row lookahead. IDs must be canonical positive safe integers; invalid cursors and duplicate/unknown query parameters return safe 400. Every page is owner-scoped. The UI saves exclusive boundaries for Previous; Refresh and mutations reload the current page. New rows have higher IDs and appear on later pages. These are live views: deletes may shorten or empty pages or change a revisited page, and Previous navigation remains explicit. There is no snapshot or total page count. Nullable fields have explicit “Set FIELD to null” checkboxes; unchecked fields follow their declared scalar type and cannot use blank as zero or null. The editor sends all declared fields on PATCH. Blank strings are invalid; string/text limits are 255/10000 characters after trimming, numeric fields must be finite, integers safe, booleans strict. JSON mutations are limited to 16 KiB. Response objects, scalar values, list limits/order and mutation echoes are checked at runtime before display. Errors are generic; failed writes retain the editor and may already have persisted, so refresh before retrying. No search, sorting controls or conflict detection are included.

Pages use relative same-origin fetch with HTTP-only cookie sessions and no token storage. Logout/session/account changes unmount private pages and invalidate pending responses even if abort is ignored. Route changes also discard resource editors and pending UI results. Navigation uses native keyboard-accessible links with current-page labels; fields, null controls, edit/delete buttons and status/error messages are labelled.

Both static manifests use an exact recognized grammar. Automatic registration refuses formatting changes, executable suffixes, dynamic imports, mismatched API/page registrations, modified shared runtime, unsafe directories, symlinks and existing new artifacts. Existing pages may be edited and must remain syntactically valid regular TSX files. The same project lock stages and validates all six artifacts before exclusive publication, backs up both manifests, and restores only unchanged generator-owned files on failure. See the framework resource documentation for crash recovery; recovery includes `previous-pages.tsx` as well as `previous-manifest.ts`. Run with the app stopped, review output and migrations, then typecheck/build/test before restarting.

This creates a typed owner model with cascade deletion, strict Create/Update schemas, a static handler, and entries in `src/web/resources.ts`, included by the app routes. `/api/tasks` supports GET/HEAD (up to 100 rows per page, ordered id, optional exclusive `after` cursor and `X-Has-More` header) and POST; `/api/tasks/:id` supports GET/HEAD, PATCH and DELETE. Existing session middleware supplies ownership through typed context. Mutations require the configured fixed Origin, including PATCH/DELETE. Item queries include owner and id; other users see 404. IDs must be canonical positive safe integers. POST/PATCH accept at most 16 KiB JSON. Responses contain only id and declared scalar fields.

Fields allow string, text, integer, number, boolean; suffix `?` means optional on create and nullable storage. Strings/text trim and enforce lengths 1–255/1–10000. Integers must be safe integers; numbers finite; no coercion or extra keys. Updates are partial and reject empty bodies/objects. Database-nullable `?` fields accept explicit null on create or PATCH; PATCH `{ "body": null }` clears the column to SQL NULL, and later GETs return null. Omitted PATCH fields remain unchanged. Nonnullable fields reject null on create and PATCH. Omitted nullable create fields output null. This differs from `make:request`, where `?` permits omission/undefined only. There are 1–32 fields; names and equivalent DB column collisions are validated. URLs use lower-kebab plus `s` (Category becomes `/api/categorys`).

Scaffolding is offline by default but requires the app's installed/linked Bundle validation module and unchanged generated User/Session/auth/router/CLI/app routes plus exact resource manifest. Customizing prerequisites opts out; older apps need reviewed manual integration. No existing artifacts are overwritten. `--migration` checks production/baseline guards before publication, then generates a diff for review without applying it. If that later diff step fails, the complete scaffold is preserved; fix the cause and retry `make:migration`.

All artifacts are staged/validated and published with exclusive hard links. Failures roll back only unchanged generator-owned files and restore the previous manifest exclusively. Concurrent user edits remain untouched, with recovery files retained when necessary. Run generation with the app stopped; this is not crash-atomic. After interruption, confirm no generator runs, inspect `src/web/.bundle-resource-*` (`recovery.json`, staged files, `previous-manifest.ts`) and current destinations, back up user edits, then finish or undo only the generator's unchanged files. Restore a backup only into an absent manifest; reconcile concurrent edits manually. Remove staging and `src/web/.bundle-resource.lock` after reconciliation, then typecheck/test before restarting. Never delete a stale lock without inspecting state. Trusted directories and hard-link support are required.

## Frontend acceptance for framework maintainers

From the Bundle checkout, run `bun run scripts/verify-app.ts`. It builds the generated output before checking rendered account forms, then runs migrated SQLite API acceptance. For actual React DOM interactions against the generated SQLite handlers, set `BUNDLE_ACCEPTANCE_DOM_MODULE=/absolute/path/to/jsdom/lib/api.js` to a host-installed jsdom module. This optional test tool is not an app dependency. Tests cover minimal session listing, revocation/current refusal, malformed dates/shapes, stale reads/mutations across Account/logout boundaries, expiry, password validation, wrong-password session preservation, rotation, second-device revocation, old/new login, pending account changes and confirmed expiry, plus register/create/list/logout/login, account isolation, StrictMode, hostile response shapes, fixed errors, session expiry, and delayed reads/writes across account changes. If omitted, the script explicitly reports DOM interactions as skipped; the rendered and API checks still run.

Set `BUNDLE_ACCEPTANCE_FIREFOX=1` to additionally launch a private headless Firefox profile against generated Vite/API servers and run real HTTP interactions (Python 3, Firefox and loopback sockets required; `BUNDLE_FIREFOX` overrides the binary). This validates cookie restoration after refresh, escaping, registration, account isolation, Notes creation/listing and logout/login. No Chrome is used. An explicitly requested browser run fails if sockets or Firefox are unavailable. `BUNDLE_ACCEPTANCE_NODE_MODULES=/absolute/path/to/installed/app/node_modules` reuses dependencies and does not verify installation.
